Decoupled **Drupal** backend (`drupal/`) + **Nuxt** frontend (`frontend/`), built on the [iqual CMS](https://github.com/iqual-ch/blokkli-starterkit-sw-experiment) platform with the [Blökkli](https://docs.blokk.li/) visual editor. The frontend consumes Drupal via GraphQL. Local development runs entirely inside **DDEV**.

* [Live URL]({{ url }}/)
* [Local URL](https://{{ name }}.ddev.site/)

## Prerequisites

- **WSL2**: For usage with Windows it is required to use WSL2.
- **Docker**: Required if you wish to use the integrated development environment.
- **DDEV**: Make sure you have [ddev](https://ddev.readthedocs.io/) installed on your system.
- **VS Code**: Recommended for developing code with the provided settings and extensions.
- **Git Access**: To clone the repository and manage version control.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> _Make sure to install the right version of DDEV for your system. We recommend DDEV on Windows with WSL2 + Docker Desktop ([docs](https://ddev.readthedocs.io/en/stable/users/install/ddev-installation/#windows)). Additionally it is required to set up SSH authentication in DDEV so Drush can access the remote environment using `ddev auth ssh -f $HOME/.ssh/YOUR_SSH_KEY`._

## Quick Start

1. **Clone** the repository
2. **Install**: `make install` — finishes by starting the Nuxt dev server in the foreground, so keep it running
3. **Launch**: `make launch` from a second terminal (to log into the admin backend directly: `make login`)

Everything runs through DDEV — always prefix `composer` / `drush` / `bun` / `npm` with `ddev`. The `Makefile` is the source of truth for build, test, and lint targets.

## Local Development

To start developing locally, launch a Visual Studio Code environment customized for this project by running:

```bash
make code
```

### Useful Commands

* `make install`: Install the project with runtime locally.
   > Use `DDEV_SNAPSHOT=latest` flag to restore the latest `ddev` snapshot.
* `make stack`: Build & deploy both the Drupal backend and frontend.
   * `make drupal`: Build & deploy Drupal backend locally. Runs daemonized.
   * `make frontend`: Build & deploy the frontend application. Runs in foreground.
* `make update`: Update the project (Composer, Drupal database & config, Bun).
* `make test`: Run project tests (PHPUnit, Vitest, …).
* `make lint`: Lint the project (PHPCS, PHPStan, Prettier, …).
* `make beauty`: Beautify & fix code in project.

> [!TIP]
> _Run `make help` for a list of all available commands. Some commands are also available as tasks in VS Code._

> [!TIP]
> _Use `ddev snapshot` to take database snapshots and run `make install DDEV_SNAPSHOT=latest` to re-install from the latest database snapshot._

## Installing Bundles

A default installation only includes core functionality. Additional ICMS features are available as bundles:

```bash
make bundle-news                        # Install a single bundle
make bundle-news DEFAULT_CONTENT=true   # With demo content
make bundle-all                         # Install all available bundles
```

To list available bundles:

```bash
composer suggest iqual/icms_core --list
```

## Project Scaffolding

Project infrastructure files (`Makefile`, `.ddev/`, CI/CD workflows, this README, …) are managed by [`iqual/drupal-nuxt-platform`](https://github.com/iqual-ch/drupal-nuxt-platform) and are updated with the Composer dependencies — do not edit managed files directly, they are overwritten on every scaffold run. Project-specific documentation belongs in the `./docs` directory. To change the scaffolding configuration use:

```bash
ddev composer project:init
```

